  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Ginn's eight shutout innings gave them something to work with early, and they used it.",
    "what_this_means": "J.T. Ginn went 8.0 innings and allowed 1 earned run against Philadelphia, pitching the kind of game that gives an offense room to breathe. The Athletics took that room and sprinted through it. They scored in four of their first five innings. Leading 4\u20130 after the first, extending to 7\u20130 by the third. And never looked back. This was not a narrow escape or a late rally. The game was decided by the third inning. Rooker and Gelof each drove in three runs. The offense converted four of six runners in scoring position and did not waste a single inning with traffic on the bases. When a starter gives you that kind of control, you capitalize. They did.",
    "what_to_watch": "The fragility everyone keeps waiting to expose did not show itself in Philadelphia. This was not a struggle or a grind. It was a team moving cleanly through early innings and burying a division rival by the time most games are still close. Pitching held. Bats arrived early and often. No one had to carry the load alone. The ERA ticked down to 4.57. The OPS climbed to .734. That's the direction. The question now is whether this margins can hold when the next opponent doesn't hand you a four-run lead in the first. The formula works when it starts this way.",
